# Product vision

Full reference: `docs/product-vision.md`. Read it before adding features, data models, or integrations.

## Hard rules

- North star: the simplest way for a business owner to know who to contact next, why, and what to say. When requirements are ambiguous, build the smaller thing.
- Shortlist does not send emails or LinkedIn messages, automate outreach, export contact lists, manage pipelines, or expose configuration (scoring weights, prompts, providers, custom fields).
- Every account uses the same fixed fields: company, website, industry/size/location, why it fits, signal + source, decision-maker (name, title, LinkedIn), verified email only, outreach angle, LinkedIn message draft.
- Never fabricate signals, contacts, fit reasons, or personalization. If a drop can't be filled, deliver fewer, say so, carry the shortfall forward.
- Drops come from shared sector libraries in Supabase, ranked per user. No per-user discovery API calls per drop. Contacts are looked up and verified only at delivery.
- An account goes to at most 3 users per quarter (configurable). Never deliver skipped domains, the user's own domain, or repeats.
- Only use data licensed for customer-facing delivery. Do not use Clay-sourced data in Shortlist without written approval from Clay.
- Use "accounts", never "leads". One price per plan; no per-unit pricing. Plans and caps are config, not hard-coded.
- Supabase holds product data; Attio gets customer (member) data only, never members' prospect lists.
- Weight outcomes (replied, booked) above fit ratings in matching. Keep scoring simple and explainable; no ML platform.
- If a request conflicts with these rules, state the tradeoff briefly instead of silently expanding scope.
